      "text": "To find the product, we multiply each fraction together: $$ \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}.$$ Denote the numerator of the first fraction by $1,$ and the numerator of the last by $50.$ Then, $50 = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 49\\  .$ So, $$\\frac{50}{53} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 49}{53} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{49}{53}.$$Thus we multiply numerator and denominator of the last fraction by $4,$ and it follows $$\\frac{50}{53} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{4}{53}.$$Thus, $$\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}$$becomes $$\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{4}{53} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 4.$$Note that each of the numerators from $\\frac{2}{5}$ through $\\frac{4}{3}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ is the product of $2$ and a number; similarly, each of the denominators from $\\frac{5}{7}$ through $\\frac{10}{5}$ and $\\frac{4}{5}$ is the product of $2$ and a number. This is because each numerator and denominator is $\\frac{2}{9}$ times an odd number. Thus, the product of all the numerators is $(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\dots \\cdot 2 = 2^{49})$ times $1 * 3 * 4 \\dots 49,$ and the product of all the denominators is $4^{49}$ times $1 * 3 * 5 \\dots 95.$ Finally, $$\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}$$turns into $$\\frac{(2^{49})}{(4^{49})} \\cdot \\frac{1 * 3 * 4 \\dots 49}{\\frac{2}{9} * 1 * 3 * 5 \\dots 95} = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{120}}.$$ The answer is: \\frac{1}{120}",
